A BOUNDARY THAT AUTO-DERIVES A TAINT LABEL MUST MEAN WHAT THE LABEL MEANS.
AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP derived host_secret from env_read, and env_read catalogued
two different things: ambient CONFIGURATION reads whose values may carry a
credential (os.getenv, System.getProperty, std::env::var, process.env), and host
DESCRIPTION or identity reads that are not secrets in any ordinary sense
(runtime.GOOS, os.uname, navigator.platform, platform.system, pwd.getpwnam).

Census over the shipped catalogues: 134 of 195 rows were the second kind -- 114
pure platform description, 16 user/process identity. Every host-secret-* claim
therefore counted description reads as secret flows: 48 of 85 adjudicated flows
at 22.9% precision, the weakest family in measurement 0001, and 51 of 59
situations in 0004.

THE CATALOGUE WAS ALREADY DISTORTING ITSELF TO COPE, which is what makes this a
vocabulary defect rather than an imprecision. python.yaml deliberately withheld
getpid / cpu_count / times -- "env_read rows auto-derive host_secret TAINT
SOURCES ... and a pid is not a secret" -- while go.yaml rowed GOOS, Getwd and
Executable. One boundary value, two membership rules, two shipped files: the
apex/peer hit in the fundamental-concept audit, which alone is grounds to
deprecate. All four leakage tests fired.

THE SPLIT IS OF THE BOUNDARY, NOT THE LABEL, AND NOT A PER-ROW OVERRIDE.
A rename leaves one boundary meaning both things and breaks three shipped
example claims for nothing; a per-row taint_label would let the row AND the
boundary each decide, which is one fact in two homes. CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES is
the registry-backed thing, so it is where the distinction belongs.
host_info_read -> host_description. host_description is NOT a weaker
host_secret: a claim naming one does not match flows carrying the other, and
generic-taint-claims.yaml now ships host-description-no-network as its own
question -- telemetry and fingerprinting, not credential exfiltration.

130 rows moved across 13 catalogues; ONE was removed rather than moved.
os.getrandom was filed under env_read and derived a host_secret source. It is a
CSPRNG read, not an environment read under any reading, and the same file
already keeps os.urandom out for its own still-live reason. After the split the
auto-derived source population is 87 host_secret / 183 host_description / 579
untrusted_input across 17 languages.

Family D (user and process identity, 16 rows) FOLDS INTO host_info_read rather
than getting a third boundary -- owner's call. "This app sends the username to a
third party" is a genuinely distinct claim, but 16 rows with no measured
consumer demand is a speculative vocabulary entry; declaring it later is cheap.

THE TWO VOCABULARIES ARE NOW PINNED TO EACH OTHER, so a boundary cannot be added
without deciding what it derives, and a catalogue cannot use a boundary nobody
declared -- which would otherwise be silently inert (no auto-source, no
auto-sink, no display grouping, no error). Both arms are asserted at the
derived-source layer with a control population, because "everything is
host_description now" would pass a one-arm test.

17 tests pinned the old placement and are rewritten to preserve their own
subject rather than deleted: the python-climb rows assert enumeration
(INV-zubuh's question, unchanged) at the new boundary, and the scoped-path rows
were always about the SEPARATOR fold, not the boundary value.

Owner-ratified 2026-08-25. Census and concept audit:
docs/surveys/env-read-boundary-census.md. Decision: ADR-0016's boundary table.
NO precision target is promised -- the band ruling stands until the owner
re-ranks on measurement 0005, which is what prices this.

